course: dictionary
lesson: 14868
title: Readable
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Readable is an adjective, and it has two main meanings. The first is easy and pleasant to read: «The story was so readable that I finished it in one afternoon.» The second is clear enough to be read, like handwriting: «Her handwriting was readable, even in pencil.»
explain: Use the words around readable to choose the right meaning. If the sentence is about a book you enjoyed, it means easy and pleasant. If it is about writing, a sign or a map, it means clear enough to read. Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
explain: The word is built from read and -able. The ending -able can mean able to be done, so readable means able to be read. Its opposite is unreadable, which means too hard or messy to read.
fact: Readable means easy and pleasant to read.
fact: Readable also means clear enough to be read, such as «readable handwriting».
fact: «The map was readable even after the rain.» Here readable means clear enough to read.
fact: In computing, readable code is code that people can easily understand.
fact: Readable comes from read and -able, so the opposite is unreadable.
exercise: «Her handwriting is readable, so the teacher can mark it quickly.» What does readable mean here? | Very loud | Written in a foreign language | *Clear enough to be read | Hard to see because it is wet
exercise: «The book was so readable that Sam finished it in two days.» What does readable mean here? | Full of pictures | Too heavy to carry | Written in a secret code | *Easy and pleasant to read
exercise: Which word means the opposite of readable? | Pleasant | Clear | *Unreadable | Bright
test: «The essay was neat and readable.» What does readable mean in this sentence? | Sung out loud | Shiny and new | Full of numbers | *Clear enough to be read
test: In computing, what does «readable code» mean? | Code with no words in it | Code written in colour | *Code that is easy for people to understand | Code that only computers can run
exam: Which sentence uses readable to mean easy and pleasant to read? | *«The novel was so readable that I could not put it down.» | «The handwriting was readable on the form.» | «The map was readable after the rain.» | «The sign was readable from across the road.»
